why 1 11/16 CROSSING The tenor dips G–F♯–G, a neighbor figure, under the bass's held G: a crossing of one sixteenth.
why 2 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor share B♭: the soprano falls to A in the dominant and the tenor moves on through G to D.
why 2 1/4 SIX_FOUR The cadential ⁶₄ comes on beat 2 and its V on beat 3, so the half cadence lands in the middle of the bar, where the phrase ends.
why 3 11/16 CROSSING Again the tenor dips B♭–A–B♭ under the bass's held B♭, a neighbor figure for one sixteenth.
why 4 1/2 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and alto fall D–C and G–F a fifth apart, the soprano an eighth earlier. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
why 6 0 UNRESOLVED_NCT The tenor holds C over the bar line into the B♭ chord and falls to B♭: a 9–8 suspension, prepared, tied and resolved by step.
why 6 5/8 LEAPS After its leap up the tenor turns back down by a third to E♭, then steps up to F. It turns, by a skip instead of a step.
why 7 1/4 SPACING At the fermata the bass drops an octave to a low F for a full cadence chord.
why 8 1/2 LEAPS The tenor walks down F–E♭–D–C–B by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 8 3/4 LEAPS The alto walks down A–G–F–E♭ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 9 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence on C minor (iv): soprano D–C, alto F–E♭, the tenor's B drops to G and the bass to C. All the resolutions point down.
why 12 5/8 SPACING Alto and tenor move apart in contrary motion: the alto leaps up to C while the tenor walks down. The gap lasts about a beat.
why 12 5/8 LEAPS The alto leaps up D–G–C to sit high under the soprano while the tenor walks down. Leaping through chord notes keeps the line clear.
why 12 11/16 SPACING The tenor keeps walking down, to G, under the alto's high C.
why 12 3/4 SPACING The widest point: the tenor down on F, the alto on C.
why 12 7/8 SPACING The alto steps down to B while the tenor holds F; the gap starts to close.
why 13 0 SPACING Still open as the cadence begins; the tenor climbs A–B♭ over the next eighths and closes it.
why 13 1/4 DOUBLING The cadential ⁶₄ of the final cadence: soprano and tenor share B♭, which falls to A and moves on. The doubling follows their lines.
why 13 3/4 RANGE The bass drops an octave to a deep D on the dominant, giving the final cadence a full, low bass.
why 13 3/4 SPACING The bass's octave drop leaves a wide gap under the tenor on the last dominant.
why 13 7/8 SPACING The same wide gap, as the tenor adds the seventh C before the final chord.
